WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.187
This is a dam made of packed
dirt, twenty-four metres tall.

00:00:08.537 --> 00:00:12.670
Water from the lake creeps through
it, and comes out of its face.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.900
Engineers score a slope
by the grip it has,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.268
divided by the grip it needs to stay put.

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:20.429
At one, it slides.

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:24.680
This dam scores one point one
two, so it's barely standing.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:28.930
There are three obvious fixes:
make it fatter, put a wall

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:32.460
in the ground under it, or bury
a strip of sand inside it.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.110
Which one saves it?

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.258
To judge a fix, you need to
know why the dam is weak.

00:00:40.608 --> 00:00:43.108
Soil holds together
because its grains press

00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:45.902
on each other, and
friction needs that press.

00:00:46.252 --> 00:00:48.972
Water in the gaps between
the grains pushes them

00:00:48.972 --> 00:00:51.499
apart, and takes away part of the press.

00:00:51.849 --> 00:00:53.336
You've seen it at the beach.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:56.706
A wall of damp sand
stands up straight, until

00:00:56.706 --> 00:00:59.723
a wave soaks its base, and then it slumps.

00:01:00.073 --> 00:01:01.884
So where is the water in this dam?

00:01:03.134 --> 00:01:04.899
Every dam leaks.

00:01:05.249 --> 00:01:08.889
Water finds its way through a
dam, under it, through the rock

00:01:08.889 --> 00:01:11.797
around it, and along any
pipe that passes through it.

00:01:12.147 --> 00:01:15.351
Here, the path that matters
is straight through the dam.

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:19.021
This is the course's seepage
solver, working out where the

00:01:19.021 --> 00:01:22.713
water goes in a dam of one
ordinary soil, standing on clay.

00:01:23.063 --> 00:01:25.896
A drop of water takes decades to cross it.

00:01:26.246 --> 00:01:29.218
This line is the water
level inside the dam.

00:01:29.568 --> 00:01:32.262
Engineers call it the phreatic surface.

00:01:32.612 --> 00:01:37.024
Where it meets the back slope, the
water comes out, about eight metres up.

00:01:37.374 --> 00:01:40.354
The Bureau of Reclamation's
manual says a dam of one

00:01:40.354 --> 00:01:43.411
soil will always do this,
however flat its slope.

00:01:43.761 --> 00:01:46.733
About two thirds of its leak
comes out of that face.

00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:50.055
So this dam leaks out of its own face.

00:01:50.405 --> 00:01:52.356
But why would that make it slide?

00:01:53.606 --> 00:01:57.971
In nineteen fifty-five, the engineer
Alan Bishop published a way to check.

00:01:58.321 --> 00:02:01.015
Draw a curve through the
slope where it might slip.

00:02:01.365 --> 00:02:04.290
Then cut the soil above the
curve into forty slices.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.331
Each slice's weight pulls it downhill,
and the soil underneath grips back.

00:02:09.681 --> 00:02:14.092
That grip is friction, and it comes
from how hard the grains press.

00:02:14.442 --> 00:02:17.442
Below the water level,
water pressure pushes up on

00:02:17.442 --> 00:02:20.294
each slice from underneath,
and the grip shrinks.

00:02:20.644 --> 00:02:24.777
Add up the grip, divide by the pull,
and that's the score for this curve.

00:02:25.127 --> 00:02:29.121
The solver tries more than two
thousand curves, and keeps the worst.

00:02:29.471 --> 00:02:33.372
On the worst one, water carries
about a third of the soil's weight.

00:02:33.722 --> 00:02:36.882
Take that water pressure
away, and the same soil on the

00:02:36.882 --> 00:02:39.991
same curve would grip more
than half again as hard.

00:02:40.341 --> 00:02:42.570
That's why this dam scores so low.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.582
The first fix most people reach
for is to make the dam bigger.

00:02:47.932 --> 00:02:50.512
Flatten the back slope,
using about a tenth more

00:02:50.512 --> 00:02:53.458
dirt, and the score rises
to one point two seven.

00:02:53.808 --> 00:02:56.780
But the water still breaks
out at the same height.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.846
And all that extra dirt has
to be dug, hauled and packed.

00:03:01.196 --> 00:03:05.933
Flatten it further, with a quarter more
dirt, and it reaches one point four two.

00:03:06.283 --> 00:03:09.063
That's still short of
one and a half, the score

00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:11.437
engineers usually want with the lake full.

00:03:11.787 --> 00:03:14.527
The manual says it
plainly: the water comes

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:16.849
out of the slope,
however flat you make it.

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:20.189
So try stopping the water instead.

00:03:20.539 --> 00:03:23.743
Drive a wall down through
the ground under the dam.

00:03:24.093 --> 00:03:27.762
The score barely moves,
to one point one four.

00:03:28.112 --> 00:03:30.792
On this clay ground, only
about a quarter of the

00:03:30.792 --> 00:03:33.314
water was going under the
dam in the first place.

00:03:33.664 --> 00:03:38.725
The wall turns that water up into the
dam, and the leak falls by just a tenth.

00:03:39.075 --> 00:03:40.840
The back slope still weeps.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.990
On sand and gravel, where
much more of the water goes

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:47.042
under, a wall like this
can be the right fix.

00:03:47.392 --> 00:03:48.599
Here, it isn't.

00:03:49.849 --> 00:03:52.821
Every dam leaks, so you
can't stop the water.

00:03:53.171 --> 00:03:55.122
Instead, give it a way out.

00:03:55.472 --> 00:03:59.412
Bury a strip of sand inside
the dam, standing just behind

00:03:59.412 --> 00:04:03.181
the crest, and join it to a
layer of sand along the base.

00:04:03.531 --> 00:04:06.571
Sand lets water through
about ten thousand times more

00:04:06.571 --> 00:04:09.289
easily than this soil,
so the water takes it.

00:04:09.639 --> 00:04:13.539
It takes up a tenth of the
dam, and it adds no soil:

00:04:13.539 --> 00:04:16.141
the sand simply replaces some of the fill.

00:04:16.491 --> 00:04:18.441
Engineers call this a drain.

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:21.253
Now watch the water level inside the dam.

00:04:21.603 --> 00:04:25.271
It dives into the sand strip before
it ever reaches the back slope.

00:04:25.621 --> 00:04:29.987
The back half of the dam is left
with almost no water pressure in it.

00:04:30.337 --> 00:04:33.680
A drop now gets through in
years, instead of decades.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.392
The score jumps to one point six three.

00:04:37.742 --> 00:04:41.142
The new worst curve runs
shallow, near the face,

00:04:41.142 --> 00:04:43.686
and water carries almost
none of its weight.

00:04:44.036 --> 00:04:49.098
With no water in it at all, the same
dam would score one point six seven.

00:04:49.448 --> 00:04:51.770
The soil was strong enough all along.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.485
The water in it was the trouble, and
the drain takes that trouble away.

00:04:59.335 --> 00:05:04.304
And here's the twist: the drained dam
leaks about two and a half times more.

00:05:04.654 --> 00:05:08.091
But it leaks through a way
out the engineer chose.

00:05:09.341 --> 00:05:10.827
That should feel familiar.

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:15.960
The back slope of a dirt dam is that
sandcastle wall, with the tide inside it.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:19.510
The drain keeps the tide
out of the wall, so the

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:22.487
grains in the slope press
with their full weight.

00:05:23.737 --> 00:05:27.220
Draining is one of several
ways engineers manage seepage.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:33.070
They cut it off, make its path longer,
drain it, filter every exit, weigh

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.440
down the ground where it comes out,
and watch it all with instruments.

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.950
The Bureau of Reclamation
says dam design has moved

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.781
from trying to stop
seepage to controlling it.

00:05:43.131 --> 00:05:48.146
Today, a strip of sand like this is standard
practice in dams built of one soil.

00:05:49.396 --> 00:05:51.811
But all that water comes out somewhere.

00:05:52.161 --> 00:05:55.783
Where it leaves the ground, it
pushes on the grains it passes.

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:58.827
What if it pushes hard
enough to carry them away?
